<h2>Deal breakdown (EXAMPLE numbers, not a real property)</h2>
<p>We made up this deal to show how the math works. It does not describe a real property or a real offer.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Purchase:</strong> $150,000 · <strong>Rehab:</strong> $40,000 · <strong>ARV:</strong> $260,000</li>
<li><strong>Loan:</strong> 90% of the purchase plus 100% of the rehab = $175,000 at 9.99% interest only, 2 points, $1,500 in lender fees</li>
<li><strong>Hold:</strong> 6 months, $550/mo for taxes, insurance, and utilities</li>
<li><strong>Sell:</strong> 5% commission plus 1.5% closing costs</li>
</ul>
<p>Running it through our calculator:</p>
<div class="tbl"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Item</th>
<th>Amount</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Interest (6 months)</td>
<td>$8,741</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Points + lender fees</td>
<td>$5,000</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Buying closing costs (2%)</td>
<td>$3,000</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Holding costs</td>
<td>$3,300</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Selling costs</td>
<td>$16,900</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Total project cost</strong></td>
<td><strong>$226,941</strong></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Estimated profit</strong></td>
<td><strong>$33,059</strong></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Cash you put in</td>
<td>$35,041</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>ROI on cash</td>
<td>94.3% (about 189% annualized)</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p><strong>The catch:</strong> under the 70% rule, the max offer is $260,000 × 70% − $40,000 = <strong>$142,000</strong>. This deal pays $8,000 more than that and still pencils out, mostly because the lender covers so much of the cost. If the ARV comes in $25,000 low or the rehab runs over, that margin vanishes quickly. Treat the 70% rule as a smoke alarm, not a law. If a deal sets it off, recheck your ARV comps before you go higher.</p>
<p>Try it with your own numbers: <a href="{pre}calculator.html">Flip profit calculator</a></p>
<h2>Quick tip: ask how the rehab money is charged</h2>
<p>Some lenders charge interest on the full rehab budget from day one. Others charge only on the draws you've actually taken. On a $40,000 rehab at about 10%, that difference can be worth $1,000 or more over six months. Ask before you sign. (Our calculator assumes the worst case, so your real number may come in better.)</p>
<p>That's it for week one. Hit reply and tell me what you want covered next.</p>
